<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Essex</h1><p class="st-meta">527 CE – 825 CE (approx.) · capital London</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of the East Saxons north of the Thames, which controlled London for long periods and had a dynasty with unusual 'S' names.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The East <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saxons">Saxons</a> settled Essex, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middlesex">Middlesex</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hertfordshire">Hertfordshire</a>. Their royal genealogy traces kings to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aescwine-of-essex">Aescwine</a>, traditionally placed in 527, and later kings bore names beginning with S, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saeberht-of-essex">Saeberht</a>, Sigeberht and Swithred.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Essex held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> called a market of many peoples. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saeberht-of-essex">Saeberht</a> accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> around 604 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mellitus">Mellitus</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of London, but the kingdom reverted to paganism after his death until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cedd">Cedd's</a> mission in the 650s. Several kings often ruled jointly. Essex usually stood under the overlordship of Kent or <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Mercia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 825 Essex submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egbert-of-wessex">Ecgberht</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex</a>, and later fell within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a>. In the 10th century it was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England</a> as a shire, which survives as the county of Essex.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-essex.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Essex" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Hel-hama · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Essex_2.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>527 CE – 825 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>London</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Saeberht, Sigeberht the Good, Swithred</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Essex, Kingdom of the East Saxons</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=676&amp;focus=kingdom-of-essex" aria-label="Kingdom of Essex on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4677 395 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Essex lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4997 651L4962 619M4997 651L4970 663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4962 619h0M4970 663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks London. <a href="/atlas?year=676&amp;focus=kingdom-of-essex">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
